meta {
  name: Get Email Settings
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/agency/email
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("states the address mail actually goes out as", function () {
    // The most important field on this screen — "pending" and "verified" look
    // identical in a form.
    expect(res.getBody().effectiveFrom).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
  });

  test("defaults to the platform sender", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().sendingStatus).to.equal("platform");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /agency/email

  How the agency's outbound email is addressed.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:email:read`.
  Separate from `agency:branding:*` and `agency:domains:*` because the blast
  radius differs: a bad logo is cosmetic, a bad sender address stops every
  invite arriving.

  **Response `200`**
  ```json
  {
    "fromName": "Texas Holdings",
    "fromLocalPart": "hello",
    "replyTo": "service@texasholdings.com",
    "sendingDomain": null,
    "sendingStatus": "platform",
    "verifiedAt": null,
    "lastError": null,
    "effectiveFrom": "\"Texas Holdings\" <notifications@smithfamily.agency>",
    "dnsRecords": null
  }
  ```

  ## `effectiveFrom` is computed, not stored
  It is built with `buildFromHeader` in `common/mail/sender-address.ts` — the
  **same** pure helper the worker uses to build the real header. That shared
  helper is the point: it makes it impossible for this screen to display an
  address that nothing sends from.

  `sendingStatus` values: `platform` (the default — our domain, their name),
  `pending`, `verified`, `failed`.

  **Errors**
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:email:read`.
}
